                        "review": "This shooter is more hits than misses with some nice boss battles and a narrative to add some character. However, it should be noted that the game is over in just over an hour with only 3 difficulty settings and leaderboards as an incentive to keep playing.\n\nThe game opens up with a brief introduction to its universe complete with a motive and an exaggerated do-gooder protagonist. Steam and Metal is not afraid to be silly (steampunk jokes and all) and its lofty writing is smirk inducing. The hand drawn slides area nice touch to give the game some character. The in game graphics and sound are pretty average, but the game certainly tries to leave an impression with its story.\n\nThe gameplay mechanics are standard but tight. You have a health bar and limited lives. Running out of lives will send you back to restart the level, however your score/pick ups will carry over to the next level so there is a strong incentive to stay alive. Pick ups include the usual suspects: health, extra missiles, extra lives, and bonus scores, but your firepower is based on picking up gears or bullets with each tier adding another stream of bullets. Some of the level designs are well made with death riddled mazes and telegraphed danger. The boss fights feel demanding at times and keep the player on their toes. However, this game will not appeal to everyone.\n\nCompared to other shooters Steam and Metal felt a bit sluggish at times. The weapons are also very average: you fire a single stream of bullets at first, then as you collect gears you can add up to two more streams of bullets to total at three. Your missiles require you to hold down your button for a short amount of time before they can be released (they can be held while firing), which does force you to choose your targets carefully. Given the lack of weapon variety, and little strategy (in other games you have to juggle a shield or your speed increases when not firing, only one standard single player campaign etc.) this game is on the lighter side, especially when coupled with the brief campaign length.\n\nSteam and Metal really is a score driven top down shooter. Your ship can take a limited number of hits (depending on difficulty), but you can receive a bonus for not being hit at all. Accuracy, enemies killed, missles used, etc. all factor into your final score. Given that there are only 6 levels and 3 difficulty levels, the realy meat of the game will probably come through multiple plays in order to carve your name on the leader boards.\n\nClocking in at a little more than an hour (with most of the achievements thrown in) this is a top down shoot 'em up that is overall enjoyable. This game is lacking in a few areas (like variety and depth), so those that are already knee deep in shoot 'em ups and bullet hells may want to look elsewhere for their fix. However this is a neat experience for those who are in the market for a streamlined and brief experience. This game boasts dynamic boss battles, solid game play, a short campaign, and a neat little narrative mixed in the middle of all the bullets, but I would recommend waiting for a sale/bundle.\n\n\n\n",
